// error
// #2162 (owner decision 2026-08-10): a `(k, v)` pair binder is rejected for
// `for ... in ...` — one form per meaning, since `for (k, v) of m` already
// covers pairs. Unlike the string/chan rejections this ticket also adds
// (E0091, validateloop.bit), a pair binder here is not even grammatical:
// Appendix A's `for_in = IDENT "in" expression .` admits one IDENT, unlike
// `for_of`'s `( IDENT | "(" pat "," pat ")" )`, so `for (a, b) in m` fails to
// PARSE rather than reaching the checker. That parse failure already exists
// on the pinned 0.1.13 oracle, byte-identical to this compiler (verified,
// #2162) — this is not a diagnostic this tree introduces, so it belongs here
// with the corpus rather than behind tests/bit/checkerdiag.bit's registered
// gate, which is reserved for a rejection no released compiler emits.
fn main() {
  let m = map<string, int>{ "a": 1, "b": 2 }
  for (a, b) in m {
    print("${a}${b}\n")
  }
}
